      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Traditionalist theologians often employ emotionally charged language to underscore their belief that societal changes in marriage and family constitute a profound spiritual and moral crisis. They frequently characterize these changes as \"a deliberate attempt to erode the sanctity of the family unit.\" These theologians assert that such shifts not only violate divine commandments but also corrode the spiritual well-being of society. They tend to speak of a \"devastating fragmentation\" of the traditional family, portraying it as fundamental to the moral instruction and development of individuals. Further, they may describe the consequences as leading to what they perceive as inevitable \"moral decay,\" citing fear of societal collapse and widespread psychological harm. The language typically intensifies to suggest that these developing norms herald a significant and regrettable \"turning away from God's teachings,\" with a sense of urgency and concern for the repercussions on the soul's integrity and the collective moral compass.",
