    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Indeed, for many traditionalist theologians, these modern shifts are not merely different viewpoints but are seen as objectively detrimental to the foundation of civilization. They employ a core logic that is deeply rooted in theological doctrine, arguing that marriage as a sacrament, as understood by traditional teachings, serves a divine function, not merely as a civil institution. According to this logic, altering its basic definition attacks the very foundation upon which societal ethics and spiritual growth are supposedly built. They suggest that such changes lead to a loss of foundational values, replaced with relativism, leading to a slippery slope of moral decline where traditional virtues no longer serve as the benchmarks for right and wrong. Through this lens, the traditional family is depicted as a cornerstone of societal stability and moral development; thus, any substantive redefinitions are viewed as fatal blows to societal cohesion. In their discourse, they often assert that this decoupling of marriage from procreation and traditional norms not only disrespects divine authority but also leads to what they see as inevitable chaos, exacerbating social issues and individual despair. In essence, they frame the conversation not as an evolution of ideas but as a catastrophic detour from a divinely sanctioned way of life, which they view as the true savior of civilization",
